-- title:
--   Shell-window functions in the Whittaker translate span, ideal level
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a height-one prime of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$, write $F =$ `p.adicCompletion ℚ` for the associated completion of $\mathbb Q$, and let $N$ be a non-zero ideal of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$. Let $w_{2,\mathrm{base}} \colon \mathrm{GL}_2(F) \to \mathbb C$ be a function subject to four conditions: it satisfies the Whittaker transformation law $w_{2,\mathrm{base}}(\begin{pmatrix}1&x\\0&1\end{pmatrix} g) = \psi_p(x)\, w_{2,\mathrm{base}}(g)$ for all $x \in F$ and $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(F)$, where $\psi_p$ is [`NumberField.StandardAddChar.psiLocal ℚ p`](def/LanglandsTunnell_StandardLocalConstantsAt.html#L65), the local character obtained by composing the standard adelic character of $\mathbb Q$ with the additive map placing an element of $F$ at the place $p$; it is right invariant under the subgroup [`AdelicDock.localLevelOne (𝓞 ℚ) ℚ p N`](def/AdelicDock_LocalEmbedding.html#L178), that is, under those $k \in \mathrm{GL}_2(F)$ whose image under the embedding $\mathrm{GL}_2(F) \to \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q}^{\mathrm{fin}})$ placing $k$ at $p$ lies in `finiteLevelOne` for $N$, i.e. both the matrix and its inverse satisfy `IsLevelOneMatrix` for $N$; it is not identically zero; and the $\mathbb C$-span $V$ of its right translates $g \mapsto w_{2,\mathrm{base}}(gh)$, $h \in \mathrm{GL}_2(F)$, is irreducible in the sense that for every non-zero $w \in V$ the function $w_{2,\mathrm{base}}$ lies in the span of the right translates of $w$. Let $f \colon F^\times \to \mathbb C$, let $n_1, n_0 \in \mathbb Z$ and $m \in \mathbb N$, and assume $f(y) = 0$ whenever $v(y) > \exp(-n_1)$ or $v(y) < \exp(-n_0)$, and $f(yu) = f(y)$ for all $y$ and all units $u$ with $v(u) = 1$ and $v(u-1) \le \exp(-m)$. Then there is $w \in V$ with $w(\mathrm{diag}(y,1)) = f(y)$ for all $y \in F^\times$.
--
--   This is the statement that the Kirillov model of an irreducible generic representation of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Q_p)$ generated by a Whittaker vector of level $N$ contains every function on $\mathbb Q_p^\times$ supported on finitely many valuation shells and invariant under a congruence subgroup of units, in the form where the level is an arbitrary non-zero ideal rather than an exact power of $p$. It supplies the bump vectors used in the Rankin–Selberg and Kirillov-pairing arguments, and is cited in the construction of shell-character decompositions of cuspidal vectors and in vanishing criteria for Kirillov pairings.
-- source:
